1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,366
Open Resource Monitor, and you might see almost no free memory.

2
00:00:04,646 --> 00:00:05,608
Don't panic.

3
00:00:05,888 --> 00:00:07,481
That's usually on purpose.

4
00:00:08,231 --> 00:00:13,435
Windows fills spare RAM with files and apps you've used, in case you need them.

5
00:00:13,715 --> 00:00:17,733
A service called SysMain learns your habits and loads things early.

6
00:00:18,013 --> 00:00:19,527
That's the standby cache.

7
00:00:20,277 --> 00:00:22,213
Task Manager lists it as Cached.

8
00:00:22,493 --> 00:00:26,441
It isn't counted as In use, and it still counts as available.

9
00:00:27,191 --> 00:00:32,121
When an app needs room, Windows drops cached data and hands that memory over.

10
00:00:32,401 --> 00:00:34,188
Empty RAM does nothing.

11
00:00:34,468 --> 00:00:37,362
Cached RAM can make the next launch faster.

12
00:00:38,112 --> 00:00:40,426
Check two numbers on the Performance tab.

13
00:00:40,706 --> 00:00:46,998
If In use sits near your total and Committed keeps climbing toward its limit, you're really short on memory.

14
00:00:47,748 --> 00:00:51,794
The full diagnosis is in our video, Why Your PC Is Slow.

